From rolf at math.unb.ca  Tue Aug  3 20:14:24 2004
From: rolf at math.unb.ca (Rolf Turner)
Date: Tue, 03 Aug 2004 17:14:24 -0300
Subject: Re: glm model comparison
Message-ID: <200408032014.i73KEOCT028987@erdos.math.unb.ca>

You wrote:

> I have several models and I would like to see if one model is
> significantly more predictive than another.

        What does ``significantly more predictive'' mean?

>                                              Here is my sample

        Do you mean ``example''?

> Model1<-glm(Side~ABM, poisson)
> Model2<-glm(Side~FBR, poisson)
> Model3<-glm(Side~GRT, poisson)
> 
> I tried: anova(Model1, Model2, Model3, test="Chi") but the test
> does not provide probability levels.

        I dare say it doesn't.  The foregoing makes no sense.  You
        can only do a chi-squared (likelihood ratio) test in respect
        of ***nested*** models.  The test then tells you if there is
        evidence that the bigger model is an improvement over the
        smaller one.

> This is a sample, so trust the models need to remain separate and
> can not be combined (e.g., model<-glm(Side~ABM+FBR+GRT, poisson).

        I am completely mystified by the foregoing.  I strongly
        suspect it makes no sense at all.  You have three possible
        predictors for ``Side''; if they make sense individually,
        why shouldn't they make sense in combination?

        If it does make sense, please explain!

                                cheers,

                                        Rolf Turner
                                        rolf at math.unb.ca

