From mschmidt at neuro.columbia.edu  Tue Aug  3 20:43:41 2004
From: mschmidt at neuro.columbia.edu (Schmidt, Michael)
Date: Tue, 03 Aug 2004 16:43:41 -0400
Subject: Re: glm model comparison
Message-ID: <526804E77BD5324DA6A026378FD5A73E02EDE8A4@exchange2k.neuro.columbia.edu>

There are two basic research questions.  The first is whether taking a
drug (e.g., ABM, FBR, GRT) increases the likelihood of exhibiting
psychiatric symptoms while on the drug.  

The second question is whether patients are more likely to exhibit
psychiatric symptoms more often when taking one drug than another.  

The difficulty of a single model is that it is not enough to code
patients on whether they ever had a psychiatric symptom and plug in all
the drugs because the symptoms need to be time locked to taking the
drug. 

So, what if a patient took one drug at time point A and had symptoms,
but took a second drug at time point B and didn't have symptoms.  How do
you code your outcome? 

The data we have are:
1. Ever had a psychiatric symptom (0,1)
2. For each drug, a. Never took drug = 0, Took drug with no symptoms =
1, and Took drugs and had symptoms = 2.  

Our perhaps very flawed idea was to compare the t-scores of the
individual models to see if they were significantly different from each
other, either by creating a confidence interval of t-scores and
examining the overlap or by a direct test. 

Have I ventured into the realm of stupidity?  Have I missed something
simple?  Is there a multivariate test we should be considering instead?

Thanks again for your help.

