From david.jessop at ubs.com  Mon Aug  9 11:19:35 2004
From: david.jessop at ubs.com
Date: Mon, 09 Aug 2004 12:19:35 +0100
Subject: Re: Moore- Penrose inverse
Message-ID: <1429C3C95392064BA7E22C708F8417180233E515@NLDNC006PEX1.ubsgs.ubsgroup.net>

Xao,

 

There 
is no function for doing this, but using the svd () function gives you the route 
to the answer.  If you get the svd of your matrix = u * diag (d) * t(v) 
then the inverse is v * diag (1 / d) * t (u).  The secret is to set 1/0 to 
0 in the 1/d _expression_, so do something like

 

temp = 
svd (m)

k = 
temp$d > (temp$d [1] * 0.01)        ## One 
definition of which of the d's to ignore

d = 
temp$d

d [k] 
= 1 / d [k]

d [!k] 
= 0

 

inv = 
temp$v %*% diag (d) %*% t (temp$u)

 

Regards,

David Jessop

 

