Dear list, 

 

Has anyone tried to get some sort of confidence band on a neural net (say, a single hidden layer with a typical sigmoid and a weight decay penalty set by cross-validation) by bootstrapping? If so, did you encounter problems with this approach? As far as i'm aware, Professor Ripley's nnet library doesn't provide estimation of the confidence intervals. Any reference to discussion of this issue appreciated.

 

Horace W. Tso

 

From tplate at blackmesacapital.com  Mon Aug  9 17:32:52 2004
From: tplate at blackmesacapital.com (Tony Plate)
Date: Mon, 09 Aug 2004 11:32:52 -0600
Subject: Re: Bootstrapping Neural Nets to get CI
In-Reply-To: <s1174b94.032@pgn.com>
References: <s1174b94.032@pgn.com>
Message-ID: <6.1.0.6.2.20040809112254.0cefdaf0@mailhost.blackmesacapital.com>

Do you want confidence bounds on parameters (weights) or 
predictions?  Bootstrapping would be a reasonable approach to getting 
confidence bounds on predictions.  It would be difficult to calculate 
confidence bounds on parameters (weights) via bootstrapping because of all 
the symmetries in a standard feedforward neural network with hidden 
layers.  For instance, each hidden unit is interchangable, and weights 
coming into and out of a particular hidden unit can have their sign 
flipped.  This would mean any bootstrap computation of confidence intervals 
for weights would be merely computing the confidence interval for the 
arbitrarily signed value of an arbitrarily selected weight connected to the 
same input (or output).

For theoretical approaches to computing confidence bounds for weights, one 
good source is "Neural Networks for Pattern Recognition" by Chris Bishop.

hope this helps,

Tony Plate

