From cmoyer at fs.fed.us  Wed Aug 11 23:57:47 2004
From: cmoyer at fs.fed.us (Chris Moyer)
Date: Wed, 11 Aug 2004 16:57:47 -0700
Subject: mixed effect model
Message-ID: <OF86A4DA75.E59925B5-ON88256EED.0080151F-88256EED.0083A268@notes.fs.fed.us>

Hello-

I need some advice/guidance from the collective S community. I have the
following analysis that I need help with:

I am working with a large spatial and temporal data set. The dataset is
comprised of polygons that are placed at random (according to a sampling
mechanism with inclusion probabilities approximately equal to those of
Simple Random Sampling) on the landscape (n=250, N=1300). Within each
polygon the number of acres of disturbed vegetation was measured (this
comprises the spatial component of the dataset). Each polygon was measured
(total number of acres of disturbance) at each of seven times between 1972
and 2002 (this comprises the temporal component of the dataset). After the
initial sample was drawn, within each polygon a post-stratification of
ownership (two levels) and location in the watershed (two levels) was made,
both of these I view as grouping variables.

I am trying to answer the following question: Is there a difference in
forest vegetation disturbance rates (total acres) across all polygons
across time in ownership and location nested within ownership?

I have fit the following mixed effects model in S-Plus (S V. 6.1; Win2000)
and believe it to be correct for the data and the question being asked:

lme(fixed=Area~TimePeriod, data=my.df,
random=~TimePeriod|Ownership/Location)

I have model this (loosely) after the various examples of the Orthodont
dataset analysis in the S-Plus help and Pinheiro & Bates (2000) . In
essences I have multiple subjects (experimental units) measured at
different intervals in time that are grouped by another factor(s). The
difference is that I want to make a statement across all subjects with
respect to the grouping variables.

My questions:

1. Have I developed the model correctly to answer the question I am asking?
2. How can a factor (TimePeriod) be both a fixed effect and a random
effect?
3. Is there a better way (more appropriate) to analyze this dataset?

Thanks in advance for any help.

Chris Moyer
Fish Biologist

From jadhavpr at vcu.edu  Wed Aug 11 13:25:58 2004
From: jadhavpr at vcu.edu (Pravin)
Date: Wed, 11 Aug 2004 09:25:58 -0400
Subject: Re: mixed effect model
