From lorenz at usgs.gov  Fri Aug 13 12:53:26 2004
From: lorenz at usgs.gov (David L Lorenz)
Date: Fri, 13 Aug 2004 07:53:26 -0500
Subject: Re: Same plots for 20 dataframes
Message-ID: <OF1E4702BA.598F3C6C-ON86256EEF.00453A2E-86256EEF.0046CFD5@cr.usgs.gov>

Sjoerd,
  Without knowing the structure of your data it is difficult to give you an
exact example.  But I can give you some hints.
  The objects function will give you a vector of the names of objects in
your project chapter. So for example if all of the data frames were named
like d1.X , where X is any combination of letters of numbers and periods,
the command would be d1.df <- objects(regexpr.pattern='^d1\.',
class='data.frame'). This would assume that all the column names among the
data frames are identical.
  If the column names are dependent on X, then the substring function can
extract the value of X. An example would be X <- substring(d1.df[1], 4).
  The column names could be generated using the paste function. Continuing
the example: Colnames <- paste("ind.", X, ",awzg,b", sep=""). The
graphsheet name could be X for example.
  The for function could be used to create the loop.  Here's the basic
idea:

for(i in objects(as above)) {
  # 1 extract X
  # 2 make first column name
